            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>This Week's Phenomenon: Cross-Border Teams' Month-End Reconciliation "Collective Breakdown"</h2>
<p>This week, Yiyu Digital Media's customer service received multiple urgent inquiries focusing on a recurring issue: top-up records, platform actual spend, and invoice amounts don't match during monthly reconciliation. A tool app team reported that they topped up $5,000, Meta's dashboard showed $4,800 in spend, but the invoice received was $4,650. "That's a $350 difference. We asked platform support for three days, and they said it was due to exchange rate fluctuations and account adjustment periods."</p>
<p>This discrepancy is not an isolated case. We've observed that overseas ad teams generally face three major challenges in reconciliation:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Top-up credit delays</strong>: After credit card/PPV top-ups, funds don't arrive instantly, with a 1-3 business day "funds in transit" period</li>
<li><strong>Platform spend measurement differences</strong>: Various platforms (Meta, Google, TikTok) have different spend statistical timing points</li>
<li><strong>Invoice cycle misalignment</strong>: Platform invoices typically lag behind the spending cycle, with inconsistent currency conversion timing</li>
</ul>
<h2>Root Cause Analysis: Three Time Gaps Causing Number Discrepancies</h2>
<p>Why do top-ups, spend, and invoices always "calculate separately"? The core issue lies in three time gaps.</p>
<p><strong>First Gap: Top-up Confirmation Delay</strong></p>
<p>When topping up via credit card or PayPal, fund transfers, bank processing, and platform crediting each have delays. For example, with Meta, credited funds may take 24-48 hours to display as available balance. During this period, if you continue advertising, you're spending from "estimated credit," not actual credited funds.</p>
<p><strong>Second Gap: Spend Measurement Variance</strong></p>
<p>Platform spend measurements have fundamental differences. Meta calculates from when ads genuinely generate impressions, Google from valid ad clicks, and TikTok's billing cycle differs slightly. More complexly, some platforms make "account adjustments" at month-end, deducting invalid traffic fees—these adjustments often don't immediately notify advertisers.</p>
<p><strong>Third Gap: Exchange Rate and Invoice Cycle Discrepancy</strong></p>
<p>Overseas platforms primarily settle in USD, but advertiser financial accounts may be in RMB. Exchange rates when invoices are issued often differ from top-up rates. In a case Yiyu Digital Media handled this week, exchange rate differences alone caused a discrepancy of over $200.</p>
<h2>Yiyu Recommendations: Three Steps to Establish Unified Reconciliation SOP</h2>
<p>For the above issues, we recommend overseas teams establish standardized reconciliation processes:</p>
<h3>Step 1: Allow Buffer in Top-up Links</h3>
<ul>
<li>Record "initiation time" and "crediting time" when topping up</li>
<li>Keep a 10% buffer above budget to avoid campaign interruption from delays</li>
<li>Prioritize instant-credit top-up methods where available (such as locally-supported payment options)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Use Platform Dashboard as Spend Authority</h3>
<ul>
<li>Record each platform's "current spend" from the dashboard daily</li>
<li>Don't extrapolate from mid-month data at month-end—wait for platform settlement completion (typically 3rd-5th of the following month) before reconciling</li>
<li>Monitor platform "account adjustment" notifications and trace discrepancy sources promptly</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 3: Record Invoice Reconciliation by Currency</h3>
<ul>
<li>Upon receiving invoices, compare platform spend first in invoice currency</li>
<li>When conversion is needed, lock the exchange rate at a single point in time to avoid errors from multiple conversions</li>
<li>If discrepancies exceed 3%, proactively contact platform support to request detailed reconciliation statements</li>
</ul>
<h2>This Week's One-Line Recommendation</h2>
<p>Reconciliation isn't something to do only at month-end—establish ledgers from the moment of top-up, separating "funds in transit" and "estimated spend" records. Yiyu Digital Media's financial reconciliation services can help overseas teams achieve unified management and anomaly alerts for multi-account top-ups, spend, and invoices. Welcome to consult.</p>
